  "prompt": "Find the common rule that maps an input grid to an output grid, given the examples below. Generate a python script that takes a grid as input and transforms it according to the this rule. Anticipate that this program will be applied to several grids, not only the shown test grid.\n\nYour script must define exactly one callable entrypoint:\ndef solve(grid):\nwhere `grid` is passed as a list of lists of integers (list[list[int]]) and the function returns the transformed grid in the same format.\n\nExample 1:\n\nInput:\n6 0 6\n0 6 6\n6 0 6\n4 0 4\n0 4 4\n4 0 4\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\nOutput:\n8 8 8\n8 0 8\n8 8 8\n\nExample 2:\n\nInput:\n2 0 0 3 0 0 7 0 7 1 0 0\n2 0 0 3 0 0 0 7 0 1 0 0\n0 2 2 0 3 3 7 0 7 0 1 1\nOutput:\n7 0 7\n0 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nExample 3:\n\nInput:\n3 0 0 4 0 4 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0 0\n0 3 3 4 4 4 0 2 2 0 8 8 0 1 1\n0 3 0 4 0 4 0 2 0 0 8 0 0 1 0\nOutput:\n4 0 4\n4 4 4\n4 0 4\n\nExample 4:\n\nInput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n3 0 0\n0 3 3\n3 0 0\n2 0 0\n0 2 2\n2 0 0\n8 0 0\n0 8 8\n8 0 0\nOutput:\n0 7 7\n7 7 0\n7 0 7\n\nBelow is a test input grid.\n\nInput:\n0 5 0\n5 0 5\n0 5 0\n0 3 0\n3 0 3\n0 3 0\n6 0 6\n6 6 0\n6 0 6\n0 4 0\n4 0 4\n0 4 0\n0 8 0\n8 0 8\n0 8 0\n\nYour final answer should just be the python script itself, no other text or markdown.",
  "raw_response": "def solve(grid):\n    import numpy as np\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Detect block size and arrangement\n    # Try horizontal and vertical arrangements\n    blocks = []\n    \n    # Check if blocks are stacked vertically (same width)\n    # Find block height by looking at distinct color regions\n    # Try to find n x n blocks\n    \n    # Determine block size\n    for bs_r in range(1, rows+1):\n        for bs_c in range(1, cols+1):\n            if rows % bs_r == 0 and cols % bs_c == 0:\n                nr, nc = rows // bs_r, cols // bs_c\n                n = nr * nc\n                if n < 2:\n                    continue\n                blks = []\n                for i in range(nr):\n                    for j in range(nc):\n                        b = [row[j*bs_c:(j+1)*bs_c] for row in grid[i*bs_r:(i+1)*bs_r]]\n                        blks.append(b)\n                # Check each block uses exactly one non-zero color\n                valid = True\n                for b in blks:\n                    colors = set()\n                    for r in b:\n                        for v in r:\n                            if v != 0:\n                                colors.add(v)\n                    if len(colors) != 1:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    continue\n                # Find the block with unique pattern\n                patterns = []\n                for b in blks:\n                    p = tuple(tuple(1 if v != 0 else 0 for v in r) for r in b)\n                    patterns.append(p)\n                for idx, p in enumerate(patterns):\n                    if patterns.count(p) == 1:\n                        return blks[idx]\n    return grid",
  "script": "def solve(grid):\n    import numpy as np\n    rows = len(grid)\n    cols = len(grid[0])\n    \n    # Detect block size and arrangement\n    # Try horizontal and vertical arrangements\n    blocks = []\n    \n    # Check if blocks are stacked vertically (same width)\n    # Find block height by looking at distinct color regions\n    # Try to find n x n blocks\n    \n    # Determine block size\n    for bs_r in range(1, rows+1):\n        for bs_c in range(1, cols+1):\n            if rows % bs_r == 0 and cols % bs_c == 0:\n                nr, nc = rows // bs_r, cols // bs_c\n                n = nr * nc\n                if n < 2:\n                    continue\n                blks = []\n                for i in range(nr):\n                    for j in range(nc):\n                        b = [row[j*bs_c:(j+1)*bs_c] for row in grid[i*bs_r:(i+1)*bs_r]]\n                        blks.append(b)\n                # Check each block uses exactly one non-zero color\n                valid = True\n                for b in blks:\n                    colors = set()\n                    for r in b:\n                        for v in r:\n                            if v != 0:\n                                colors.add(v)\n                    if len(colors) != 1:\n                        valid = False\n                        break\n                if not valid:\n                    continue\n                # Find the block with unique pattern\n                patterns = []\n                for b in blks:\n                    p = tuple(tuple(1 if v != 0 else 0 for v in r) for r in b)\n                    patterns.append(p)\n                for idx, p in enumerate(patterns):\n                    if patterns.count(p) == 1:\n                        return blks[idx]\n    return grid",
